    "id": "p36-q6-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "6",
    "prompt": "$ABCD$ is a cyclic quadrilateral with $AD$ parallel to $BC$. The diagonals $AC$ and $BD$ intersect at $P$. Prove that $\\angle APB=2\\angle ACB$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$\\angle APB=2\\angle ACB$.",
      "worked": "Let $\\angle ACB=\\alpha$.\n\n$\\angle BDA=\\alpha$ (angles subtended by chord $AB$), and $\\angle DAC=\\alpha$ (alternate angles, $AD\\parallel BC$).\n\nIn $\\triangle APD$, the exterior angle $APB$ equals the sum of the opposite interior angles:\n$$\\angle APB=\\angle PAD+\\angle ADP=\\alpha+\\alpha=2\\angle ACB.$$ ",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
